   {'title':'MCP allowlists in enterprise managed settings','url':'https://github.blog/changelog/2026-08-06-mcp-allowlists-in-enterprise-managed-settings','source_type':'security release','published_or_updated':'2026-08-06','why_selected':'GitHub now provides centrally managed, fail-closed MCP allow/deny policies across Copilot app, CLI, and VS Code.','usefulness':'It is an immediately usable reference for a client MCP admission policy.','what':'Enterprise settings can allow or deny MCP servers by canonicalized remote URL, exact local command/arguments, or name; malformed or unverifiable configurations are blocked.','matters':'Tool discovery turns an integration into effective agent authority. An allowlist makes that authority reviewable before it reaches developer machines.','use':'Maintain a small approved-server inventory; match remote URLs or exact commands, not friendly server names, and specify an owner and renewal date.','caveat':'This is GitHub-specific enforcement. Names are explicitly not a security control, and policy coverage is limited to supported clients.','action':'Inventory every MCP server in use and classify it approved, experimental, blocked, or unknown.'},
